  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warming trend will bring above average high temperatures in the \nmid to upper 80s by late week, along with dry conditions.\n\nThe Nor'easter continues to weaken today with just a weak sfc \nreflection and limited moisture as it slowly traverses the nearshore \nwaters off of the northern New England coast. High pressure builds \nover eastern CONUS from the Great Lake region the first part of the \nweek, allowing dry conditions and a rather substantial warming trend \nbeginning today and continuing through the end of the week. Highs \ntoday will range from the upper 60s/around 70F on the Eastern Shore \nto the mid to upper 70s for mainland VA and NC. Tuesday will be a \nfew degrees warmer, flirting with the 80s for southern portions of \nthe area. Widespread 80s Wednesday and potentially near 90F Thursday \nand Friday are likely with the Eastern Shore seeing temps a few \ndegrees cooler each day. The sfc and upper-air synoptics support dry \nwx through most of the work week. By late Friday into the weekend, \nsome sort of southern stream or elongated frontal system could bring \nprecip chances back to the region. Models continue to disagree on \ntiming and evolution of any potential system; thus, NBM PoPs are \nonly 20-50% Friday through the weekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Coastal flooding impacts continue to lessen with \neasing winds through this morning's high tide cycles. 1-2 more \nrounds of mainly minor flooding is possible through this evening, \nmainly on the Lower Maryland Eastern Shore. \n\nCoastal flood impacts continue to wind down across the local area \nthis morning. Will allow Coastal Flood headlines on the tidal \nRappahannock/Potomac sites to expire on time this morning. It will \ntake some time for water to move out of the upper Chesapeake Bay, \nand accordingly, a few locations on the Lower Maryland Eastern Shore \nwill need another round of Advisories through this afternoon's high \ntide cycle to cover residual minor flooding in the middle Chesapeake \nBay. Beyond that timeframe, while tides will remain elevated through \nmidweek due to continued higher astronomical tides in association \nwith the lunar cycle, only nuisance to near-minor tidal levels are \nanticipated through midweek.",
